%`-

require KPbuild 4799

reset all

silent = t

txt font 4
txt centre
txt c -20 "KnotPlot Rotation Tutorial"
txt push
txt expire 12
txt font 3
txt c -40 "click on the ? button on the bottom right of this window for more information"
txt pop

txt on

func 1 <tut/rotate.kps

3dop ipos 133 10
show axes euler
knot
cyl-rad=.3


txt left
txt lineskip -25
txt fcolour royalblue .6
txt font 3

txt 10 10 "current euler angles:"




txt fill on
txt 15 a2 "about z" "~abo z"
txt 15 a "about y" "~abo y"
txt 15 a "about x" "~abo x"
txt fill off
txt font 4
txt 10 a1.5 "change embedding"
txt font 3

txt fill on
txt 15 a2 "rotate k" "~rot k"
txt 15 a "rotate j" "~rot j"
txt 15 a "rotate i" "~rot i"
txt fill off
txt 10 a "camera"
txt fill on
txt 15 a1.5 "rotate z" "~rot z"
txt 15 a "rotate y" "~rot y"
txt 15 a "rotate x" "~rot x"
txt fill off
txt 10 a "world"

txt fill on
txt 15 a1.5 "rotate dir 1 1 1 z" "rotate dir 1 1 1 z"
txt 15 a "rotate dir -1 -1 -1" "rotate dir -1 -1 -1"
txt 15 a "rotate dir 1 1 1" "rotate dir 1 1 1"
txt 15 a untran untran
txt fill off

txt font 4
txt 10 a1.5 "change view"
txt font 3


txt font 4
txt fill on
txt right
txt -14 14 ? "help popup tut/rotate.kps handoff"


txt font 3
txt fill on
txt select off
txt -15 60 fullscreen "full toggle"
txt -15 a  grid "show ~grid"
txt -15 a  axes "show ~axes"
txt fill off
txt -10 a toggle:

txt fill on
txt -15 a2 "rerun demo" <tut/rotate.kps
txt -15 a "new knot" knot
txt -15 a "gimble lock"  "txt group 102 off;par1=0;~clob;idle;<tut/rotate-gimble.kps"
txt -15 a "print angles" "txt group 102 off;par1=0;~clob;idle;echo;echo;echo;view euler"

alias ~update "if par1 == 1 then ~last"

txt push
txt fill on
txt centre
txt group 102
txt c+150 35 "reset" "par0=1;~update"
txt c+50 35 "reverse" "test paradj 0 -1 0;~update"
txt c-150 35 "slower" "test paradj 0 .8 0;~update"
txt c-50 35 "faster" "test paradj 0 1.25 0;~update"
%txt fill off
%txt -10 a speed:
txt c 10 "stop rotation" "txt group 102 off;par1=0;~clob;idle"
txt pop
txt group 102 off


par0=1

par1 = 0  % whether currently rotating

alias ~rot "txt group 102 on;~setlast ~rot $0;par1=1;~blurt $0 \ppar0;idle {rotate $0 \ppar0}"

alias ~abo "txt group 102 on;~setlast ~abo $0;par1=1;~blurta $0 \ppar0;idle {about $0 \ppar0}"

alias ~setlast "alias ~last {$0 $1}"

alias ~clob "txt delete group 108"
alias ~blurt "~clob;txt push;txt group 108;txt fill off;txt cent;txt font 4;txt c 88 {repeatedly executing command:};txt c 62 {rotate $0 $1};txt pop"
alias ~blurta "~clob;txt push;txt group 108;txt fill off;txt cent;txt font 4;txt c 88 {repeatedly executing command:};txt c 62 {about $0 $1};txt pop"

alias ~testdir "line dir $0 $1 $2;line;rotate dir $0 $1 $2 $3"
alias ~td "~testdir \rr+-2 \rr+-2 \rr+-2 \ri2"

silent = f

exit

%`+
KnotPlot Rotation Tutorial

This demo illustrates the options for rotating things in KnotPlot.  It also explains the 
difference between the `rotate' and `about' commands, that may at first glance appear to do the
same thing. 

The `rotate' command changes the view only and does not affect the actual embedding of the knot.
It will not change the coordinate values of the vertices in the knot. `rotate' has two versions:

  --- x y or z for rotations about the world coordinate axes
  --- i j or k for rotations about the camera (viewer) coordinate axes, think of the ij plane
      as being the coordinate system of your screen

The view may also be changed using the "virtual trackball" within KnotPlot. Try the following:

   --- click down with the left mouse in the view window and drag.
   --- try pressing x y or z before clicking down to perform world centered rotations (it might
       be best to click the `stop rotation' button first if the knot is auto-rotating.  it'll be
       easier to see what is happening).
   --- or press i j or k before clicking to perform camera centered rotation. 

With any of the latter options, make sure the view window has keyboard focus (clicking once
anywhere in the window will give it keyboard focus).

In contrast, the `about' command changes the knot's coordinates and has no effect on the view. 

To work the demo, try the following:

  --- click on any of the `rotate' or `about' buttons on the left hand part of the window. 
  --- while the knot is rotating or abouting, rotate the knot using the virtual trackball as
      explained above.
  --- observe the current euler angles and when you feel like it, click on `print angles' to see
      the KnotPlot commands that can reproduce the current view. If you want to save these 
      commands to a file, type the following into the KnotPlot Command Window:
      view angles > myangles.txt
      Note the spaces before and after the >.   To reproduce the view, type the command
      <myangles.txt
      into the KnotPlot Command Window. `myangles.txt' is arbitrary, use whatever name you want.
  --- turn off both the axes and grid, then click on one of the `rotate' x, y or z buttons.  
      then click on the corresponding `about' button. you should see little or no difference
      in the animation when switching between the two.  only when the grid or axes are on will
      the difference be apparent. 
  --- the `gimble lock' button illustrates the problem with using pitch, roll and yaw (euler 
      angles) to specify an orientation.  click down near the X that appears and drag the mouse
      in small circles around the X.  note that the last of the euler angles experiences huge
      discontinuous changes as you do this.  this singularity in the pitch/roll/yaw coordinate
      system is the source of the `gimble lock' problem afflicting robot arms and camera tripods.
